WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.220
When working a sew-as-you-go method

2
00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:07.100
of joining seams,
it can be difficult to locate which strand

3
00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:11.660
to pick up, especially when working
on the standard gauge machine.

4
00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:16.100
Creating a neat seam can be a challenge.

5
00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:21.020
We've used this technique to knit socks
in a flatbed knitting machine

6
00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:27.260
sew-as-you-go eliminates sewing
up the side seams by hand.

7
00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:34.440
Here's one method. If you loop a strong,
smooth, contrasting yarn along each row as

8
00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:39.580
you knit, it's much easier
to identify the loops to pick up.

9
00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:42.260
I'm using a crochet bedspread cotton,

10
00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:46.300
but you could use any yarn
that will slide out easily.

11
00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:50.140
I've tied one into the cotton through
the end of my double eye needle.

12
00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:54.860
You could also use a darning needle.
On the carriage side,

13
00:00:54.890 --> 00:01:01.540
drop the needle behind the working yarn
and loop the cotton over the working yarn.

14
00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:02.980
Don't pull it tight.

15
00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:06.820
You don't want to distort
the edge stitch of your knitting.

16
00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:10.280
Knit over and back.

17
00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.180
Pick up your needle from the front.

18
00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:16.620
Drop the needle back behind the working

19
00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.490
yarn and loop the cotton
over the working yarn.

20
00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.100
Again, knit over and back.
Be consistent.

21
00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:30.020
How you're picking up that needle and
putting it over behind the working yarn.

22
00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:33.820
Keep repeating this up the entire
edge that's going to be joined.

23
00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:40.920
The contrast color loops identify the
knots and the loops are clearly visible.

24
00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:47.740
Use your transfer tool to pick up the loop
of your knitting and hang it

25
00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:52.960
on the machine, doubling up
the edge stitch of your knitting.

26
00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.850
If you pick up the cotton
at the same time, it won't hurt.

27
00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:03.120
Just might be a little bit harder to get
that cotton out when you're all done.

28
00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:31.890
Once the piece is done,
just pull out that crochet cotton.

29
00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:37.380
Gently pull the seam to pop the stitches
in place, and you'll have a nice cabled

30
00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:41.800
look that is secure
and tidy for your project.

31
00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.160
It's also comfortable in socks.

32
00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.220
Another idea is the bobby pin trick.

33
00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.100
Similar to the previous method,

34
00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:58.820
we'll isolate the loops that will be used
to seam the piece. On the carriage side,

35
00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:02.060
carefully hang a bobby
pin on the working yarn.

36
00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:03.940
Don't pull down.

37
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.700
You might also want to hold the yarn as
it's fed into the carriage so you don't

38
00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:11.940
get a big loop on the edge of your
knitting because of the marker.

39
00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:14.610
Carefully knit to the left.

40
00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.180
Check to make sure that the pin isn't
sticking up, and then knit back.

41
00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:22.860
Hang another bobby pin knit over.

42
00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:28.300
Check to make sure it's not going to get
caught up in your carriage, and knit back.

43
00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:30.220
Repeat this for the entire length

44
00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:37.200
of the seam, identifying each one
of the loops from every other row.

45
00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.220
When it's time to join this section,

46
00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:59.260
pick up each loop by the pin
and hang it on the machine needle.

47
00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:02.060
Knit over and back.

48
00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:03.300
Keep repeating.

49
00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:05.140
Pick up the loop.

50
00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:07.580
Hang it on the machine again.

51
00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.320
You're doubling up those edge stitches.

52
00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:14.300
You're not adding any stitches
you're just doubling up seaming.

53
00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:18.600
Carefully remove the pin each time.

54
00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:40.120
You'll have a neat and tidy seam with no
guesswork about which strands to pick up

55
00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:47.240
try both of these methods
and see which one you like.

56
00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:55.880
They will improve your
sew-as-you-go seaming.

